PUBLIC RESPONSE: The correct choice is E. With heterogeneous treatment effects (explicitly flagged in the setup), the usual overidentification statistics no longer have the required property: a rejection does not guarantee violation of exclusion/exogeneity, because the instruments may simply be recovering different LATEs. The Anderson-Rubin statistic tests the value of the structural parameter (not instrument validity). The Hansen J statistic is the standard overID test and is invalidated by heterogeneity. A plain t-test has no overID interpretation at all. Hence none of the listed procedures satisfies the stated guarantee.

(The one-known-valid instrument can still be used to construct a valid specification test, but it is not any of A–C.)

}

